Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 17:56:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(tools): os.fs.grep on a single file died with `spawn
 ENOTDIR`
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`os.fs.grep` passed the caller's `path` straight to the child process `cwd`
and hardcoded `.` as ripgrep's search target. That only works when `path` is
a directory: pointing it at one FILE — a natural reading of "grep this file",
and what a model does unprompted — made `spawn` fail with a bare `ENOTDIR`
naming no argument.

Hit twice in real runs on a local 27B, both times as
`{pattern: "// <<NEXT>>", path: ".../index.html", outputMode: "count"}`.
The model recovered by falling back to whole-file reads, so the run survived
but burned steps.

`resolveSearchRoot` now splits the path: a directory searches itself, a file
searches from its parent with the basename as the target. A path that does
not exist is left alone so ripgrep's own "no such file" message survives
instead of being replaced by a spawn error.

Also adds the Qwen 3.8 27B GGUF to the local model catalog, from the
`ggml-org` conversion because it ships the matching `mtp-` draft model in
the same repo.

Verified by replaying the exact failing tool call: status error -> ok.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
